\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S13}{\ignorespaces Hypothetical short-position profit and loss (P\&L) at the fixed ten-trading-day horizon, on May 12, 2026, with 17 calendar DTE remaining. Each row uses 3{,}000 paths. Monetary values are in dollars per share. The columns $\Delta $P\&L (paired P\&L difference) and mean $|\Delta P|$ (mean absolute price difference) compare each variant with the direct surface on identical paths. Parentheses give the paired Monte Carlo standard error of mean $\Delta $P\&L. A more negative 5\% expected shortfall (ES$_{5\%}$) means larger losses in the lower tail. Paired bootstrap standard errors of the change in ES$_{5\%}$ from the uncoupled to the full factor were \$0.15 for the GS put, \$0.24 for the GS call, \$0.13 for the LLY put, and \$0.18 for the LLY call.}}{32}{table.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:dynamic_ablation}{{S13}{32}{Hypothetical short-position profit and loss (P\&L) at the fixed ten-trading-day horizon, on May 12, 2026, with 17 calendar DTE remaining. Each row uses 3{,}000 paths. Monetary values are in dollars per share. The columns $\Delta $P\&L (paired P\&L difference) and mean $|\Delta P|$ (mean absolute price difference) compare each variant with the direct surface on identical paths. Parentheses give the paired Monte Carlo standard error of mean $\Delta $P\&L. A more negative 5\% expected shortfall (ES$_{5\%}$) means larger losses in the lower tail. Paired bootstrap standard errors of the change in ES$_{5\%}$ from the uncoupled to the full factor were \$0.15 for the GS put, \$0.24 for the GS call, \$0.13 for the LLY put, and \$0.18 for the LLY call}{table.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S14}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the full factor with the direct surface after ten trading days, with numerical diagnostics, for each evaluation seed. Each row uses 1{,}000 paths. Monetary values are in dollars per share. Mean $|\Delta P|$, mean $\Delta $P\&L, and its paired standard error compare the full factor with the direct surface. Outside is the percentage of path dates before expiration on which strike/spot lay outside the 0.8 to 1.2 range of the calibration data. Floor is the largest percentage of path dates after entry at the variance floor, taken across the five IV variants. Tree change is the largest absolute price change from 201 to 401 tree steps across the five variants. It uses the first twenty paths after ten and twenty trading days.}}{33}{table.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
